Negative‑emission CCS： These facilities remove co2 from the atmosphere or from biomass, so the overall effect is less CO₂ in the air than before.

Neutual-emission: These facilities produce energy (electricity, hydrogen, heat) without adding extra CO₂ to the air. They are “near” neutral because a small amount of CO₂ still escapes(5-20%).

90% of the co2 is peeled, the energy vector is pure

Positive-emission: These facilities make fuels like liquefied natural gas, coal‑to‑liquids, or oil sands products. Even with CCS, the carbon in the fuel will eventually go into the air when the fuel is burned. So they still have positive emissions – but much less than without CCS.

